        "id": 1129199
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-13T09:20:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not super clear if we should care here:\n\"\nWhen the LPTIM is running with an asynchronous clock, reading the LPTIM_CNT register may\nreturn unreliable values. So in this case it is necessary to perform two consecutive read accesses and verify that the two returned values are identical.\n\nIt should be noted that for a reliable LPTIM_CNT register read access, two consecutive read\naccesses must be performed and compared. A read access can be considered reliable when the\nvalues of the two consecutive read accesses are equal.\"",
      "revId": "b9b87f421fdb0645b86a217300bb3ee73afb0ef4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f6c1827_34c463d5",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/clock-stm32h7.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 317,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000016
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-14T12:05:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Useful reminder ! \nI had seen this but I was kind of confuse about it (ie how a single register read can be non-coherent) ... thinking about this, they meant that this is a *binary* *counter* asynchronously clocked (as opposed to some kind of gray-code counter for example, so when incrementing, it changes multiple *bits* non-atomically). I\u0027m adding some kind of helper then.",
      "parentUuid": "30e0711d_e8640861",
      "revId": "b9b87f421fdb0645b86a217300bb3ee73afb0ef4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a5f8c34e_7393dfca",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/clock-stm32h7.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 319,
      "author": {
        "id": 1129199
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-13T09:20:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why -1? (delay_us / LPTIM_PERIOD_US will already round down, right?)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 319,
        "startChar": 58,
        "endLine": 319,
        "endChar": 62
      },
      "revId": "b9b87f421fdb0645b86a217300bb3ee73afb0ef4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "49bc0f09_e367db2f",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/clock-stm32h7.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 319,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000016
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-14T12:05:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "TL;DR because it\u0027s a free-running counter which is already started.\n\nLet\u0027s imagine delay_us is a whole number of lptim ticks (e.g 40),\nthe LPTIM counter might be anywhere between the beginning and the end of the current 125us tick (represented by \u0027cnt\u0027), so when reaching cnt + 40, the delay would anywhere between 40 ticks and 40 ticks + 124.99999 us.\nSo to get a delay strictly lesser than the requested timer event, we need to decrease it to get between (39 ticks and 39 ticks + 124.99999 us)\n\nExperimentally without -1, on average I\u0027m oversleeping by 70us\nSo hopefully my explanation is not flawed",
      "parentUuid": "a5f8c34e_7393dfca",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 319,
        "startChar": 58,
        "endLine": 319,
        "endChar": 62
      },
      "revId": "b9b87f421fdb0645b86a217300bb3ee73afb0ef4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
